            58.5,
            340.22808837890625,
            553.4990844726562,
            393.1123046875
          ],
          "caption": "Figure 1. Comparative analysis of agent architectures on a complex geospatial query. (1) ReAct’s iterative, step-by-step process lacks a global strategy, leading to chaotic, redundant tool calls and incomplete solutions. (2) Plan&Execute adheres to a rigid, pre-determined plan, showing no capacity for correction even when encountering errors, ultimately failing the task. (3) HTAM demonstrates a structured, hierarchical decomposition, ensuring a logical progression from data acquisition (Layer 1) to analysis (Layer 2) and final synthesis (Layer 3), leading to a coherent and complete solution. Note that in (1) and (3), each tool-call denotes a thought-action-observation process.",
          "crop_file": "./images/figure-01-p002-01.png",
          "crop_rect": [
            45.0,
            63.0,
            567.0,
            399.11
          ]
        }
      ],
      "page_image": "source_pages/page-002.jpg"
    },
    {
      "number": 3,
      "text_length": 4711,
      "blocks": 7,
      "images": 3,
      "draw_clusters": 3,
      "captions": [
        {
          "kind": "figure",
          "number": 2,
          "bbox": [
            58.5,
            221.60011291503906,
            553.4990234375,
            285.44329833984375
          ],
          "caption": "Figure 2. Conceptual visualization of the planning processes of ReAct, Plan&Execute, and HTAM, inspired by gradient descent optimization. Starting from the same initial state, each architecture navigates the problem space differently. (a) ReAct takes noisy, stepby-step actions, analogous to stochastic gradient descent [4]; its path is highly reactive and can be inefficient. (b) Plan&Execute commits to a single, direct trajectory based on an initial global plan, similar to vanilla gradient descent [3], which can be brittle and fail if the initial direction is flawed. (c) HTAM operates in structured stages, providing a stable yet adaptive path analogous to mini-batch gradient descent [1], enabling efficient and robust convergence to the solution.",
          "crop_file": "./images/figure-02-p003-01.png",
          "crop_rect": [
            45.0,
            63.0,
            567.0,
            291.44
          ]
        }
      ],
      "page_image": "source_pages/page-003.jpg"
